+Here the sets contain certified option types; new option types are reported separately as option expansion. A zero initial-option denominator is reported as N/A. For transitions whose certificate context remains valid, rank-monotonicity violation is $\mathrm{RMV}=N_{\rm rank\ nondecrease}/N_{\rm valid\ rank\ transitions}$, where a nondecrease means the child rank is not strictly smaller than the parent rank. The safe-only fallback rate is the fraction of episodes ending in a certified safety controller without a certified task-completion tail. Let $N_{\rm ICG+}$ be the number of decision classes whose ICG is positive under the exact oracle or certified positive interval, and $N_{\rm late}$ the number for which no compatible public continuation is committed by the certified deferral deadline. The ICG deadline-miss rate is $\mathrm{IDM}=N_{\rm late}/N_{\rm ICG+}$. Let $N_{\rm sign}$ count oracle-decidable classes, $N_{\rm resolved}$ those for which the interval classifier returns a sign, and $N_{\rm match}$ those for which that sign is correct. Report conditional sign accuracy $\mathrm{ICGAcc}=N_{\rm match}/N_{\rm resolved}$ together with resolution coverage $N_{\rm resolved}/N_{\rm sign}$ and the all-class correct-sign fraction $N_{\rm match}/N_{\rm sign}$. Unresolved interval cases are not silently counted as correct. Let $N_{\rm pub}$ count positive-ICG classes for which a common public SCO is independently certified; public-option coverage is $\mathrm{POC}=N_{\rm pub}/N_{\rm ICG+}$. Conditional temporal-schedule success is $\mathrm{TSS}=N_{\rm DC}/N_{\rm sched}$, where $N_{\rm DC}$ counts fixed-branch temporal instances for which the dynamic-controllability checker returns a strategy. These metrics are N/A when their denominators are zero.
+
+Let $N_{\rm critical}$ be the number of capability-critical observation/mode classes identified by the exact finite oracle or exhaustive intervention analysis on the declared finite support, and $N_{\rm retained}$ the number retained by the finite selector. On large instances with incomplete discovery, report recall on the discovered critical subset explicitly as a diagnostic, not as a lower bound on full recall. If the full candidate universe has known size $M$, a conservative (possibly loose) lower bound is $N_{\rm retained,discovered}/M$; otherwise full recall is unresolved. Critical-mode recall is $\mathrm{CMR}=N_{\rm retained}/N_{\rm critical}$. Scenario reduction ratio is $\mathrm{SRR}=1-|\widehat\Omega|/|\Omega_{\rm candidate}|$, with both sets defined over the same joint-mode universe. Certification coverage is $\mathrm{CCov}=N_{\rm cert}/N_{\rm test}$, where $N_{\rm cert}$ counts initial supports for which the verifier returns \texttt{CERTIFIED} within budget; \texttt{UNKNOWN} is not counted as certified. Empirical false-safe rate is
 \begin{equation}
 \mathrm{FSR}=\frac{N_{\rm false}^{\rm exec}}{N_{\rm cert}^{\rm exec}},
 \label{eq:fsr}
 \end{equation}
 where numerator and denominator count simulator executions, not initial supports; only executions whose ground-truth trace remains inside the declared model envelope enter this falsification rate. Out-of-envelope traces are counted separately as conformance failures. For interval-box tubes $Z_t=\prod_j[\underline z_{tj},\overline z_{tj}]$, normalized tube width is $\mathrm{NTW}=\frac{1}{Tn}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\sum_{j=1}^{n}(\overline z_{tj}-\underline z_{tj})/s_j$, where $s_j$ are fixed state scales. This measures enclosure conservatism, not soundness by itself. Lawful task-completion rate is $N_{\rm legal\ accept}/N_{\rm feasible\ oracle\ episodes}$ and is reported only where the denominator is known from the finite oracle; on larger instances report the raw lawful completion fraction without claiming a ground-truth feasible-episode denominator. Safe-only fallback rate is $\mathrm{SOFR}=N_{\rm safe-only}/N_{\rm episodes}$, and wait fraction is $\mathrm{WF}=N_{\rm wait\ steps}/N_{\rm decision\ steps}$. Critical-resource recall@K is $|\mathcal R_{\rm critical}\cap\mathcal R_{\rm topK}|/|\mathcal R_{\rm critical}|$. A zero denominator is reported as not applicable. FSR is N/A if no certified simulator execution is available. All episode-level rates are accompanied by confidence intervals and task-feasible/task-infeasible subsets are reported separately.
+
+\paragraph{Task stop duration (diagnostic).}
+For an episode, let $\Delta t_k$ denote the executed decision-interval duration, let $P_k$ indicate that the mission remains pending, and let $v_k$ be the observed ego speed at the left endpoint. Report the sampled task stop duration
+\begin{equation}
+T_{\rm stop}=\sum_k \Delta t_k\,\mathbf 1[P_k=1,\ v_k\leq v_{\rm stop}],
+\end{equation}
+with a fixed, declared speed threshold (e.g., $0.2\,\mathrm{m/s}$) and a common episode horizon. Partition this duration by independently established resource availability (blocked, available, or unknown). Unknown task status and horizon truncation are reported separately; stop time is not a proof of deadlock or causally attributable delay. A stop while resources are available may still be necessary under other constraints. Lawful completion and continuation-certificate metrics remain primary.
 
 \paragraph{Ablation configurations.}
 Each variant uses the same evaluation instances and compute limits as the full method. Forecast-only ablations do not alter the verifier's declared uncertainty support unless the ablation explicitly removes the independent verifier.
